: 55209461, 'GUID': 'https://en.coin-turk.com/?p=207932', 'PUBLISHED_ON': 1764606028, 'PUBLISHED_ON_NS': None, 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/79/55209461.jpeg', 'TITLE': 'Bitcoin Faces Tough Start to December, Setting Off Ripple Effects in Crypto Market', 'SUBTITLE': None, 'AUTHORS': 'Fatih Uçar', 'URL': 'https://en.coin-turk.com/bitcoin-faces-tough-start-to-december-setting-off-ripple-effects-in-crypto-market/', 'SOURCE_ID': 79, 'BODY': 'Bitcoin continued to decline, reaching new lows and affecting the crypto market. Weak investor risk appetite despite confirmed interest rate cuts added pressure. Continue Reading: Bitcoin Faces Tough Start to December, Setting Off Ripple Effects in Crypto Market The post Bitcoin Faces Tough Start to December, Setting Off Ripple Effects in Crypto Market appeared first on COINTURK NEWS .', 'KEYWORDS': 'Cryptocurrency News', 'LANG': 'EN', 'UPVOTES': 0, 'DOWNVOTES': 0, 'SCORE': 0, 'SENTIMENT': 'NEGATIVE', 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'CREATED_ON': 1764606145, 'UPDATED_ON': 1764606145, 'SOURCE_DATA': {'TYPE': '120', 'ID': 79, 'SOURCE_KEY': 'cointurken', 'NAME': 'CoinTurk News', 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/79/default.png', 'URL': 'https://en.coin-turk.com/feed/', 'LANG': 'EN', 'SOURCE_TYPE': 'RSS', 'LAUNCH_DATE': None, 'SORT_ORDER': 0, 'BENCHMARK_SCORE': 0, 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'LAST_UPDATED_TS': 1764610922, 'CREATED_ON': 1689939795, 'UPDATED_ON': 1690273097}, 'CATEGORY_DATA': [{'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 14, 'NAME': 'BTC', 'CATEGORY': 'BTC'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 15, 'NAME': 'BUSINESS', 'CATEGORY': 'BUSINESS'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 37, 'NAME': 'MARKET', 'CATEGORY': 'MARKET'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 210, 'NAME': 'MACROECONOMICS', 'CATEGORY': 'MACROECONOMICS'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 211, 'NAME': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY', 'CATEGORY': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY'}]}, {'TYPE': '121', 'ID': 55210048, 'GUID': 'https://www.cryptopolitan.com/?p=871023', 'PUBLISHED_ON': 1764606025, 'PUBLISHED_ON_NS': None, 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/43/55210048.jpeg', 'TITLE': \"India has reportedly ordered smartphone makers to pre-install the government's Sanchar Saathi cybersecurity app on all new devices within 90 days\", 'SUBTITLE': None, 'AUTHORS': 'Hannah Collymore', 'URL': 'https://www.cryptopolitan.com/india-to-pre-install-state-cybersecurity-app/', 'SOURCE_ID': 43, 'BODY': 'India has joined a small group of countries mandating pre-installed government applications on smartphones. Apple’s manufacturing operations in India now account for more than 14% of the company’s global iPhone production, but the government’s new directive could run the company out of the market, resulting in the loss of both a major customer base and a key manufacturing hub. India’s mandatory app India’s government now reportedly requires all smartphone manufacturers to pre-install a state-owned cybersecurity app on new devices. The order was issued on November 28 and gives companies 90 days to ensure the Sanchar Saathi app comes pre-loaded on all new phones, with no option for users to remove it. Major smartphone manufacturers, including Apple, Samsung, Vivo, Oppo, and Xiaomi, which together account for most of India’s smartphone market of over 1.2 billion subscribers, are expected to comply. For devices already in the supply chain, manufacturers must push the app to phones through software updates. The government is insistent on the app because it is needed to combat what it describes as serious threats to telecom cybersecurity. These threats include duplicate or fake IMEI numbers that enable scams and network misuse. The IMEI is a unique identification number assigned to every mobile phone that can be used to block stolen devices from accessing networks. The directive has so far faced pushback from privacy advocates and technology companies. Technology lawyer Mishi Choudhary stated that the government’s decision effectively removes user consent as a meaningful choice. Choudhary pointed out that similar concerns were raised when Russia mandated pre-installation of the state-backed Max messenger app in August. Apple’s internal policies explicitly prohibit installing any government or third-party applications before a smartphone reaches consumers. The company is responsible for roughly 4.5% of India’s 735 million smartphones, with Android devices making up the rest. Apple also clashed with Indian regulators over government apps in 2017 when it delayed implementing an anti-spam app due to concerns about sending call log data to Indian authorities. The Sanchar Saathi app The Sanchar Saathi app was launched in January and serves multiple cybersecurity functions. Firstly, it allows users to track and block lost or stolen smartphones across all telecom networks using a central registry. Users can also identify and disconnect fraudulent mobile connections through the platform. Since its launch, the app has been downloaded more than 5 million times and has helped recover over 700,000 lost phones, including 50,000 in October alone. Government data shows the app has blocked more than 3.7 million stolen or lost mobile phones and terminated over 30 million fraudulent connections. Also on November 28, the Department of Telecommunications issued separate orders requiring messaging apps, including WhatsApp, Telegram, Signal, Snapchat, ShareChat, JioChat, and others, to maintain continuous binding to active SIM cards. The government says this feature is being misused from outside the country to commit cyber fraud. The government argues that requiring continuous SIM verification will make it harder for criminals to operate fake accounts remotely, as every session will require an active and verified SIM card. The messaging app order means that these services will only work if the SIM card remains present and active in the device. For web-based versions, users will be automatically logged out every six hours and must re-authenticate through QR code pairing. These platforms have been classified as Telecommunication Identifier User Entities under new regulations and must comply within 90 days. Join Bybit now and claim a $50 bonus in minutes', 'KEYWORDS': 'Tech|India', 'LANG': 'EN', 'UPVOTES': 0, 'DOWNVOTES': 0, 'SCORE': 0, 'SENTIMENT': 'NEGATIVE', 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'CREATED_ON': 1764607135, 'UPDATED_ON': 1764610949, 'SOURCE_DATA': {'TYPE': '120', 'ID': 43, 'SOURCE_KEY': 'cryptopolitan', 'NAME': 'Cryptopolitan', 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://images.cryptocompare.com/news/default/cryptopolitan.png', 'URL': 'https://www.cryptopolitan.com/feed/', 'LANG': 'EN', 'SOURCE_TYPE': 'RSS', 'LAUNCH_DATE': None, 'SORT_ORDER': 0, 'BENCHMARK_SCORE': 0, 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'LAST_UPDATED_TS': 1764610951, 'CREATED_ON': 1657730129, 'UPDATED_ON': 1681205909}, 'CATEGORY_DATA': [{'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 25, 'NAME': 'EXCHANGE', 'CATEGORY': 'EXCHANGE'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 37, 'NAME': 'MARKET', 'CATEGORY': 'MARKET'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 49, 'NAME': 'TECHNOLOGY', 'CATEGORY': 'TECHNOLOGY'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 211, 'NAME': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY', 'CATEGORY': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY'}]}, {'TYPE': '121', 'ID': 55209275, 'GUID': 'https://cryptonews.com/?p=458421', 'PUBLISHED_ON': 1764605784, 'PUBLISHED_ON_NS': None, 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/52/55209275.jpeg', 'TITLE': 'Europol Authorities Bust $1.4B Cryptomixer, Seizing $27M and 12TB of User Data', 'SUBTITLE': None, 'AUTHORS': 'Hassan Shittu', 'URL': 'https://cryptonews.com/news/europol-cryptomixer-takedown-1-4-billion/', 'SOURCE_ID': 52, 'BODY': \"Europol and law enforcement agencies in Germany and Switzerland have shut down one of Europe’s largest illicit crypto-mixing operations, seizing €25 million ($27 million) in Bitcoin and confiscating more than 12 terabytes of user data. The takedown, announced on December 1, marks one of the most extensive actions yet under the EU’s ongoing effort to dismantle services that obscure the flow of criminal funds. Europol supports Germany and Switzerland in taking down 'Cryptomixer', seizing EUR 25 million in Bitcoin. This illicit mixing service facilitated money laundering of proceeds from a variety of criminal activities. Details https://t.co/d3oTlbrDzd pic.twitter.com/Qtml6nhGlX — Europol (@Europol) December 1, 2025 Six-Year-Old Crypto Laundering Service Taken Offline The operation took place between November 24 and 28 in Zurich, with Europol supporting authorities on the ground throughout the action week. Investigators seized three servers, took control of the cryptomixer(dot)io domain, and replaced the site with a law-enforcement seizure banner. According to Europol, the platform, known as “Cryptomixer,” functioned as a hybrid mixing service on both the clear web and the dark web. Since its launch in 2016, the service has processed more than €1.3 billion in Bitcoin linked to a wide range of illegal activity. Authorities say the mixer was used heavily by ransomware groups, underground cybercrime forums, and operators on dark-web markets. Source: Europol Its software pooled deposits for long, randomized periods, then redistributed funds to new addresses designed to break transaction trails. This method helped conceal proceeds of drug trafficking, weapons trafficking, payment-card fraud, and cyberattacks, allowing criminals to convert “cleaned” assets back into other cryptocurrencies or fiat currency through exchanges, ATMs, and bank accounts. Europol coordinated intelligence sharing through its Joint Cybercrime Action Taskforce and provided forensic specialists during the raids. The agency has been involved in several major anti-mixing operations in recent years, including the March 2023 takedown of ChipMixer , then the largest service of its kind. The shutdown comes as the EU tightens its anti-money-laundering framework ahead of major regulatory deadlines. Under new AML rules tied to MiCA, crypto-mixing services are banned across the bloc, and anonymity-enhancing coins such as Monero and Zcash will be prohibited by 2027. Crypto-asset service providers are required to apply strict KYC checks, identify the sender and receiver of all transfers, and conduct enhanced due diligence on transactions above €1,000. These measures aim to close regulatory gaps that have historically allowed laundering networks to operate across borders with minimal oversight. Europol Leads Wave of Digital Crime Takedowns as Mixer Scrutiny Grows The enforcement climate around mixers has intensified globally. In January 2025, a U.S. federal grand jury indicted three Russian nationals accused of running Blender(dot)io and its successor, Sinbad(dot)io, mixers the Department of Justice says were used by the North Korean Lazarus Group. A federal grand jury in Georgia has indicted three Russian nationals for operating cryptocurrency mixing services https://t.co/O4zvAPMnTQ and https://t.co/2yKHniWPLK . #Mixer #Russian https://t.co/6fgsHt1UjR — Cryptonews.com (@cryptonews) January 12, 2025 In November, a New York court sentenced Samourai Wallet co-developer Keonne Rodriguez to five years in prison after prosecutors said the service laundered more than $237 million in illicit funds. The ruling has accelerated scrutiny of privacy-focused and non-custodial crypto tools. Notably, Samourai Wallet’s chief technology officer, William Lonergan Hill, was also sentenced to four years in federal prison for his role in the mixer activities. @SamouraiWallet founders Keonne Rodriguez and William Hill are set to reverse their plea to “guilty” in a high-profile crypto privacy case, according to New York court filings. #SamouraiWallet #CryptoMixers https://t.co/8aHVgJKESf — Cryptonews.com (@cryptonews) July 30, 2025 The Cryptomixer takedown also arrives during one of Europol’s most active enforcement years in the digital-crime ecosystem. In October, European investigators dismantled a cybercrime syndicate responsible for creating more than 49 million fake online accounts. The network provided temporary SIM-based phone numbers that allowed criminals to bypass two-factor authentication and mass-produce fraudulent identities used to exploit exchanges, banks, and e-commerce platforms. Seven suspects were arrested, and hundreds of SIM servers and routers were seized. Earlier in June, Europol led raids against Archetyp Market , one of the dark web’s longest-running drug marketplaces. Europol has dismantled one of the dark web’s longest-running marketplaces, Archetyp Market, following coordinated raids across six countries. #Archetyp #Darknet https://t.co/sweGIyi2if — Cryptonews.com (@cryptonews) June 18, 2025 Authorities seized core infrastructure in the Netherlands and arrested suspects across Europe, though experts noted that operators often regroup on decentralized platforms. The post Europol Authorities Bust $1.4B Cryptomixer, Seizing $27M and 12TB of User Data appeared first on Cryptonews .\", 'KEYWORDS': 'Blockchain News|Crypto mixers|Europol|Germany|Switzerland', 'LANG': 'EN', 'UPVOTES': 0, 'DOWNVOTES': 0, 'SCORE': 0, 'SENTIMENT': 'NEGATIVE', 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'CREATED_ON': 1764605828, 'UPDATED_ON': 1764605829, 'SOURCE_DATA': {'TYPE': '120', 'ID': 52, 'SOURCE_KEY': 'cryptonews', 'NAME': 'cryptonews', 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://images.cryptocompare.com/news/default/cryptonews.png', 'URL': 'https://cryptonews.com/news/feed/', 'LANG': 'EN', 'SOURCE_TYPE': 'RSS', 'LAUNCH_DATE': None, 'SORT_ORDER': 0, 'BENCHMARK_SCORE': 0, 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'LAST_UPDATED_TS': 1764610948, 'CREATED_ON': 1657730129, 'UPDATED_ON': 1680604053}, 'CATEGORY_DATA': [{'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 14, 'NAME': 'BTC', 'CATEGORY': 'BTC'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 26, 'NAME': 'FIAT', 'CATEGORY': 'FIAT'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 37, 'NAME': 'MARKET', 'CATEGORY': 'MARKET'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 49, 'NAME': 'TECHNOLOGY', 'CATEGORY': 'TECHNOLOGY'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 211, 'NAME': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY', 'CATEGORY': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY'}]}, {'TYPE': '121', 'ID': 55209245, 'GUID': 'b7fdf1c7-c819-4687-8827-a5e3efd39a13', 'PUBLISHED_ON': 1764605779, 'PUBLISHED_ON_NS': None, 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/102/default.png', 'TITLE': 'Bitcoin champion Strategy launches ‘dollar reserve’ amid crypto sell-off', 'SUBTITLE': None, 'AUTHORS': '', 'URL': 'https://www.ft.com/content/b7fdf1c7-c819-4687-8827-a5e3efd39a13', 'SOURCE_ID': 102, 'BODY': 'Shares tumble as group warns of loss of up to $5.5bn if digital asset prices do not rebound', 'KEYWORDS': '', 'LANG': 'EN', 'UPVOTES': 0, 'DOWNVOTES': 0, 'SCORE': 0, 'SENTIMENT': 'NEGATIVE', 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'CREATED_ON': 1764605796, 'UPDATED_ON': 1764606152, 'SOURCE_DATA': {'TYPE': '120', 'ID': 102, 'SOURCE_KEY': 'financialtimes_crypto_', 'NAME': 'Financial Times (Crypto)', 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/102/default.png', 'URL': 'https://www.ft.com/cryptofinance?format=rss', 'LANG': 'EN', 'SOURCE_TYPE': 'RSS', 'LAUNCH_DATE': 1307318400, 'SORT_ORDER': 0, 'BENCHMARK_SCORE': 0, 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'LAST_UPDATED_TS': 1764610922, 'CREATED_ON': 1719248005, 'UPDATED_ON': 1719248073}, 'CATEGORY_DATA': [{'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 14, 'NAME': 'BTC', 'CATEGORY': 'BTC'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 26, 'NAME': 'FIAT', 'CATEGORY': 'FIAT'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 37, 'NAME': 'MARKET', 'CATEGORY': 'MARKET'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 50, 'NAME': 'TRADING', 'CATEGORY': 'TRADING'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 211, 'NAME': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY', 'CATEGORY': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY'}]}, {'TYPE': '121', 'ID': 55209274, 'GUID': 'https://cryptonews.com/?p=458452', 'PUBLISHED_ON': 1764605724, 'PUBLISHED_ON_NS': None, 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/52/55209274.jpeg', 'TITLE': 'Bloomberg Analysts Warn Bitcoin’s Slide Below $86K Is Just the Beginning', 'SUBTITLE': None, 'AUTHORS': 'Anas Hassan', 'URL': 'https://cryptonews.com/news/bloomberg-analysts-warn-bitcoins-slide-below-86k-is-just-the-beginning/', 'SOURCE_ID': 52, 'BODY': \"Bitcoin’s overnight plunge below $86,000 has sparked fresh warnings from Bloomberg Intelligence, with senior commodity analyst Mike McGlone cautioning that the 7% drop marks only the opening chapter of a deeper bear market correction. The stark forecast comes as crypto markets reel from their worst monthly decline since February , with exchange volumes collapsing to $1.59 trillion and bitcoin ETFs bleeding $3.48 billion in net outflows during November alone. McGlone projects Bitcoin could tumble more than 35% from current levels, potentially revisiting the $50,000 threshold last seen in 2024. His analysis cites normal market reversion, record-setting gold prices, suppressed stock market volatility, and the unlimited supply of competing cryptocurrencies as key factors supporting this bearish outlook. Source: LinkedIn Yen Carry Trade Unwind Could See Bitcoin Drop Below $75k The immediate catalyst for Bitcoin’s bearish market structure emerged from Tokyo, where mounting speculation around a December rate hike by the Bank of Japan has impacted leveraged positions. Polymarket bettors now assign a 52% probability to a 25-basis-point increase at the BOJ’s December 18-19 meeting, while bond investors place those odds even higher at 76%. “Bitcoin dumped cause BOJ put Dec rate hike in play,” BitMEX co-founder Arthur Hayes wrote Monday, noting that a USD/JPY rate between 155 and 160 “makes BOJ hawkish.” $BTC dumped cause BOJ put Dec rate hike in play. USDJPY 155-160 makes BOJ hawkish. pic.twitter.com/lG47l5cbCA — Arthur Hayes (@CryptoHayes) December 1, 2025 The connection runs deeper than surface correlation; conservative estimates peg the yen carry trade at $3.4 trillion, though realistic figures approach $20 trillion. For three decades, global markets borrowed near-zero Japanese money to fund everything from tech stocks to treasuries to Bitcoin itself. That era ended last month, which triggered a sell-off in markets. Traders are now increasingly reluctant to take directional risk without downside protection, hedging, and additional clarity about macroeconomic liquidity conditions. Data from Glassnode reveals a massive overhead supply block sitting between $93,000 and $99,000, with a second resistance layer at $101,000 to $105,000. Source: Glassnode “Every bounce into this zone faces sell-pressure from trapped buyers,” warned Laqira Protocol CEO Sina Osivand. Meanwhile, the $83,000 to $86,000 band is emerging as the new cost basis for fresh demand; if this level fails to hold, liquidity hunts toward $78,000 to $75,000 become likely. Analyst Warns Bitcoin Could Test $60-65k Support. “ Bitcoin’s drop below $90,000 is the result of a collision between the fragile market structure and weak liquidity conditions observed over the weekend, ” VALR CEO Farzam Ehsani told Cryptonews. MSCI’s anticipated decision to potentially exclude companies holding over half their assets in cryptocurrency also intensified selling pressure. The rule change would affect issuers collectively holding over $137 billion in digital assets and approximately 5% of all Bitcoin in existence, including Strategy, Marathon, Riot, Metaplanet, and American Bitcoin. “Since index funds are required to adhere to a strict basket-forming methodology, any rule change automatically triggers a review of their holdings, potentially leading to forced sell-offs,” Farzam Ehsani added. December sentiment and the crypto market’s dynamics heading into the New Year will depend partly on whether Michael Saylor’s company reaches an agreement with regulators and index firms. Strategy executives appear frustrated that their company hasn't been chosen to join America's flagship S&P 500 #Bitcoin #MichaelSaylor https://t.co/RRkYgzJC0N — Cryptonews.com (@cryptonews) November 30, 2025 Traders on Kalshi have slashed odds of Bitcoin reclaiming $100,000 this year to just 29%. Source: Kalshi Ehsani warned that if the market continues declining, Bitcoin could test the $60,000 to $65,000 range , though he noted that major institutional players might view those levels as accumulation opportunities. “Strategy is a key player in the crypto market, and its potential problems could cause Bitcoin’s price to drop by another 30%,” he added. The December 18 BOJ policy decision now stands as the critical pivot; a hike with hawkish stance could push Bitcoin toward $75,000 , while a pause might trigger a short squeeze back toward $100,000 within days The post Bloomberg Analysts Warn Bitcoin’s Slide Below $86K Is Just the Beginning appeared first on Cryptonews .\", 'KEYWORDS': 'Bitcoin News|Bitcoin', 'LANG': 'EN', 'UPVOTES': 0, 'DOWNVOTES': 0, 'SCORE': 0, 'SENTIMENT': 'NEGATIVE', 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'CREATED_ON': 1764605827, 'UPDATED_ON': 1764605828, 'SOURCE_DATA': {'TYPE': '120', 'ID': 52, 'SOURCE_KEY': 'cryptonews', 'NAME': 'cryptonews', 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://images.cryptocompare.com/news/default/cryptonews.png', 'URL': 'https://cryptonews.com/news/feed/', 'LANG': 'EN', 'SOURCE_TYPE': 'RSS', 'LAUNCH_DATE': None, 'SORT_ORDER': 0, 'BENCHMARK_SCORE': 0, 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'LAST_UPDATED_TS': 1764610948, 'CREATED_ON': 1657730129, 'UPDATED_ON': 1680604053}, 'CATEGORY_DATA': [{'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 14, 'NAME': 'BTC', 'CATEGORY': 'BTC'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 37, 'NAME': 'MARKET', 'CATEGORY': 'MARKET'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 50, 'NAME': 'TRADING', 'CATEGORY': 'TRADING'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 210, 'NAME': 'MACROECONOMICS', 'CATEGORY': 'MACROECONOMICS'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 211, 'NAME': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY', 'CATEGORY': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY'}]}, {'TYPE': '121', 'ID': 55209482, 'GUID': 'https://en.coinotag.com/bitcoin-mining-faces-harsh-downturn-as-revenues-hit-lows-and-payback-periods-extend', 'PUBLISHED_ON': 1764605601, 'PUBLISHED_ON_NS': None, 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/77/55209482.jpeg', 'TITLE': 'Bitcoin Mining Faces Harsh Downturn as Revenues Hit Lows and Payback Periods Extend', 'SUBTITLE': None, 'AUTHORS': 'COINOTAG Editorial Team', 'URL': 'https://en.coinotag.com/bitcoin-mining-faces-harsh-downturn-as-revenues-hit-lows-and-payback-periods-extend', 'SOURCE_ID': 77, 'BODY': \"The Bitcoin mining revenue decline in 2025 stems from plunging hashprices to structural lows around $35 per PH/s, rising operational costs, and Bitcoin prices dropping below $80,000, extending payback periods beyond 1,000 days and challenging even major operators' profitability. Hashprice falls sharply from $55 to $35 per PH/s, marking the harshest margin environment in Bitcoin [...]\", 'KEYWORDS': 'Analysis|Bitcoin|News', 'LANG': 'EN', 'UPVOTES': 0, 'DOWNVOTES': 0, 'SCORE': 0, 'SENTIMENT': 'NEGATIVE', 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'CREATED_ON': 1764606162, 'UPDATED_ON': 1764606163, 'SOURCE_DATA': {'TYPE': '120', 'ID': 77, 'SOURCE_KEY': 'coinotag', 'NAME': 'CoinOtag', 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/77/default.png', 'URL': 'https://en.coinotag.com/feed/', 'LANG': 'EN', 'SOURCE_TYPE': 'RSS', 'LAUNCH_DATE': None, 'SORT_ORDER': 0, 'BENCHMARK_SCORE': 0, 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'LAST_UPDATED_TS': 1764610915, 'CREATED_ON': 1686819961, 'UPDATED_ON': 1717748924}, 'CATEGORY_DATA': [{'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 14, 'NAME': 'BTC', 'CATEGORY': 'BTC'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 15, 'NAME': 'BUSINESS', 'CATEGORY': 'BUSINESS'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 37, 'NAME': 'MARKET', 'CATEGORY': 'MARKET'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 39, 'NAME': 'MINING', 'CATEGORY': 'MINING'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 211, 'NAME': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY', 'CATEGORY': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY'}]}, {'TYPE': '121', 'ID': 55209148, 'GUID': 'https://u.today/zec-is-20x-lower-than-xrp-solana-builder-breaks-silence-after-zcashs-50-crash', 'PUBLISHED_ON': 1764605580, 'PUBLISHED_ON_NS': None, 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/64/55209148.jpeg', 'TITLE': '‘ZEC Is 20x Lower Than XRP’: Solana Builder Breaks Silence After Zcash’s 50% Crash', 'SUBTITLE': None, 'AUTHORS': 'U.Today', 'URL': 'https://u.today/zec-is-20x-lower-than-xrp-solana-builder-breaks-silence-after-zcashs-50-crash', 'SOURCE_ID': 64, 'BODY': \"Zcash crashed 50% in days, yet the biggest shock may have come from Solana's Mert Mumtaz claiming ZEC is trading 20x below XRP and hinting that this gap will not survive the next market move.\", 'KEYWORDS': 'XRP', 'LANG': 'EN', 'UPVOTES': 0, 'DOWNVOTES': 0, 'SCORE': 0, 'SENTIMENT': 'NEGATIVE', 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'CREATED_ON': 1764605670, 'UPDATED_ON': 1764605671, 'SOURCE_DATA': {'TYPE': '120', 'ID': 64, 'SOURCE_KEY': 'utoday', 'NAME': 'U.Today', 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://images.cryptocompare.com/news/default/utoday.png', 'URL': 'https://u.today/cryptocompare.php', 'LANG': 'EN', 'SOURCE_TYPE': 'RSS', 'LAUNCH_DATE': 1506816000, 'SORT_ORDER': 0, 'BENCHMARK_SCORE': 49, 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'LAST_UPDATED_TS': 1764610905, 'CREATED_ON': 1657730129, 'UPDATED_ON': 1745920032}, 'CATEGORY_DATA': [{'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 46, 'NAME': 'SOL', 'CATEGORY': 'SOL'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 58, 'NAME': 'XRP', 'CATEGORY': 'XRP'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 61, 'NAME': 'ZEC', 'CATEGORY': 'ZEC'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 62, 'NAME': 'ZRX', 'CATEGORY': 'ZRX'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 211, 'NAME': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY', 'CATEGORY': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY'}]}, {'TYPE': '121', 'ID': 55209481, 'GUID': 'https://en.coinotag.com/china-renews-focus-on-banning-crypto-trading-amid-surge-in-speculative-activity', 'PUBLISHED_ON': 1764605537, 'PUBLISHED_ON_NS': None, 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/77/55209481.jpeg', 'TITLE': 'China Renews Focus on Banning Crypto Trading Amid Surge in Speculative Activity', 'SUBTITLE': None, 'AUTHORS': 'COINOTAG Editorial Team', 'URL': 'https://en.coinotag.com/china-renews-focus-on-banning-crypto-trading-amid-surge-in-speculative-activity', 'SOURCE_ID': 77, 'BODY': \"Chinese authorities have intensified their crackdown on cryptocurrency trading in 2025, reaffirming that all virtual currency activities remain illegal financial operations. The People's Bank of China (PBOC) warns of renewed risks from speculative trading, building on 2021 enforcement measures that curbed domestic exchanges and mining. PBOC's renewed warnings target virtual currencies, including stablecoins, declaring them [...]\", 'KEYWORDS': 'News', 'LANG': 'EN', 'UPVOTES': 0, 'DOWNVOTES': 0, 'SCORE': 0, 'SENTIMENT': 'NEGATIVE', 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'CREATED_ON': 1764606161, 'UPDATED_ON': 1764606162, 'SOURCE_DATA': {'TYPE': '120', 'ID': 77, 'SOURCE_KEY': 'coinotag', 'NAME': 'CoinOtag', 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/77/default.png', 'URL': 'https://en.coinotag.com/feed/', 'LANG': 'EN', 'SOURCE_TYPE': 'RSS', 'LAUNCH_DATE': None, 'SORT_ORDER': 0, 'BENCHMARK_SCORE': 0, 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'LAST_UPDATED_TS': 1764610915, 'CREATED_ON': 1686819961, 'UPDATED_ON': 1717748924}, 'CATEGORY_DATA': [{'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 6, 'NAME': 'ASIA', 'CATEGORY': 'ASIA'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 26, 'NAME': 'FIAT', 'CATEGORY': 'FIAT'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 50, 'NAME': 'TRADING', 'CATEGORY': 'TRADING'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 210, 'NAME': 'MACROECONOMICS', 'CATEGORY': 'MACROECONOMICS'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 211, 'NAME': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY', 'CATEGORY': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY'}]}, {'TYPE': '121', 'ID': 55209480, 'GUID': 'https://en.coinotag.com/celestia-tia-drops-15-in-market-slump-reversal-possible-from-liquidity-builds', 'PUBLISHED_ON': 1764605492, 'PUBLISHED_ON_NS': None, 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/77/55209480.jpeg', 'TITLE': 'Celestia TIA Drops 15% in Market Slump: Reversal Possible from Liquidity Builds', 'SUBTITLE': None, 'AUTHORS': 'COINOTAG Editorial Team', 'URL': 'https://en.coinotag.com/celestia-tia-drops-15-in-market-slump-reversal-possible-from-liquidity-builds', 'SOURCE_ID': 77, 'BODY': \"Celestia (TIA) experienced a sharp 15% decline in the past 24 hours, outpacing the broader crypto market's 5% drop. This crash stems from a breakdown below key support levels at $0.60, increased capital outflows, and reduced network activity following the recent Matcha upgrade, as indicated by data from CoinMarketCap and Token Terminal. Celestia TIA price [...]\", 'KEYWORDS': 'Bitcoin|News', 'LANG': 'EN', 'UPVOTES': 0, 'DOWNVOTES': 0, 'SCORE': 0, 'SENTIMENT': 'NEGATIVE', 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'CREATED_ON': 1764606160, 'UPDATED_ON': 1764606161, 'SOURCE_DATA': {'TYPE': '120', 'ID': 77, 'SOURCE_KEY': 'coinotag', 'NAME': 'CoinOtag', 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/77/default.png', 'URL': 'https://en.coinotag.com/feed/', 'LANG': 'EN', 'SOURCE_TYPE': 'RSS', 'LAUNCH_DATE': None, 'SORT_ORDER': 0, 'BENCHMARK_SCORE': 0, 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'LAST_UPDATED_TS': 1764610915, 'CREATED_ON': 1686819961, 'UPDATED_ON': 1717748924}, 'CATEGORY_DATA': [{'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 14, 'NAME': 'BTC', 'CATEGORY': 'BTC'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 37, 'NAME': 'MARKET', 'CATEGORY': 'MARKET'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 50, 'NAME': 'TRADING', 'CATEGORY': 'TRADING'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 118, 'NAME': 'TIA', 'CATEGORY': 'TIA'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 211, 'NAME': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY', 'CATEGORY': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY'}]}, {'TYPE': '121', 'ID': 55209478, 'GUID': 'https://en.coinotag.com/trumps-crypto-czar-david-sacks-hits-back-at-nyt-over-alleged-conflict-sensationalism', 'PUBLISHED_ON': 1764605420, 'PUBLISHED_ON_NS': None, 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/77/55209478.jpeg', 'TITLE': 'Trump’s Crypto Czar David Sacks Hits Back at NYT Over Alleged Conflict Sensationalism', 'SUBTITLE': None, 'AUTHORS': 'COINOTAG Editorial Team', 'URL': 'https://en.coinotag.com/trumps-crypto-czar-david-sacks-hits-back-at-nyt-over-alleged-conflict-sensationalism', 'SOURCE_ID': 77, 'BODY': \"David Sacks, Trump's AI and Crypto Czar, has publicly criticized The New York Times for sensationalism and factual inaccuracies in reports alleging his conflicts of interest. He divested over $200 million in crypto assets before taking office and plans legal action against the outlet for persistent misleading coverage. Trump's AI and Crypto Czar Role: David [...]\", 'KEYWORDS': 'Analysis|News', 'LANG': 'EN', 'UPVOTES': 0, 'DOWNVOTES': 0, 'SCORE': 0, 'SENTIMENT': 'NEGATIVE', 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'CREATED_ON': 1764606159, 'UPDATED_ON': 1764606160, 'SOURCE_DATA': {'TYPE': '120', 'ID': 77, 'SOURCE_KEY': 'coinotag', 'NAME': 'CoinOtag', 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/77/default.png', 'URL': 'https://en.coinotag.com/feed/', 'LANG': 'EN', 'SOURCE_TYPE': 'RSS', 'LAUNCH_DATE': None, 'SORT_ORDER': 0, 'BENCHMARK_SCORE': 0, 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'LAST_UPDATED_TS': 1764610915, 'CREATED_ON': 1686819961, 'UPDATED_ON': 1717748924}, 'CATEGORY_DATA': [{'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 37, 'NAME': 'MARKET', 'CATEGORY': 'MARKET'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 41, 'NAME': 'REGULATION', 'CATEGORY': 'REGULATION'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 211, 'NAME': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY', 'CATEGORY': 'CRYPTOCURRENCY'}]}, {'TYPE': '121', 'ID': 55209054, 'GUID': 'https://coinpaper.com/12781/december-10-ultimatum-south-korean-lawmakers-threaten-to-bypass-regulators-on-stablecoin-law', 'PUBLISHED_ON': 1764605412, 'PUBLISHED_ON_NS': None, 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/93/default.png', 'TITLE': 'December 10 Ultimatum: South Korean Lawmakers Threaten to Bypass Regulators on Stablecoin Law', 'SUBTITLE': None, 'AUTHORS': '', 'URL': 'https://coinpaper.com/12781/december-10-ultimatum-south-korean-lawmakers-threaten-to-bypass-regulators-on-stablecoin-law', 'SOURCE_ID': 93, 'BODY': \"South Korean lawmakers have issued an ultimatum to financial regulators. The ruling party demands a draft stablecoin bill by December 10, threatening to push through their own legislation if regulators fail to meet the deadline. Democratic Party lawmaker Kang Joon-hyun made the position clear. If the government fails to deliver, the Political Affairs Committee will draft its own proposal. The legislation could reach the National Assembly floor during an extraordinary session in January 2026. The Financial Services Commission responded on Monday with measured language. No final decisions have been made regarding stablecoin issuance structures, the regulator stated. Officials confirmed that discussions took place during a meeting between the ruling party and the government. Both sides agreed to expedite the drafting process. The FSC explicitly denied earlier reports about banking requirements. Claims that banks must hold at least 51% equity in stablecoin consortiums remain unconfirmed. The regulator emphasized that such details are still under review. Central Bank Clashes With Regulators Over Banking Control The dispute centers on how much control banks should have over stablecoin issuance. The Bank of Korea wants traditional financial institutions at the helm. The central bank argues banks already operate under strict regulatory oversight. Their experience with anti-money laundering protocols makes them ideal candidates. The BOK proposed that banks own majority stakes in any approved stablecoin issuer. This position has drawn criticism from both regulators and industry figures. The Financial Services Commission favors a broader approach. Regulators want multiple types of entities to be eligible to issue stablecoins. This disagreement has stalled progress for months. Late November reports suggested South Korea would end 2025 without a regulatory framework. The December 10 deadline represents a renewed push to break the impasse. Industry Voices Push Back Against Bank-Centric Model Sangmin Seo leads the Kaia DLT Foundation. He told reporters in October that the central bank's reasoning appears flawed. Restricting issuance to bank-led consortiums lacks logical foundation, according to Seo. The foundation chair proposed an alternative path forward. Clear rules for all potential issuers would serve the market better than arbitrary institutional preferences. Seo called on the Bank of Korea to publish specific guidelines. These should outline risk mitigation strategies and trustworthiness criteria for any issuer type.\", 'KEYWORDS': 'News|Crypto', 'LANG': 'EN', 'UPVOTES': 0, 'DOWNVOTES': 0, 'SCORE': 0, 'SENTIMENT': 'NEUTRAL', 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'CREATED_ON': 1764605479, 'UPDATED_ON': None, 'SOURCE_DATA': {'TYPE': '120', 'ID': 93, 'SOURCE_KEY': 'coinpaper', 'NAME': 'Coinpaper', 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/93/default.png', 'URL': 'https://coinpaper.com/newsfeed', 'LANG': 'EN', 'SOURCE_TYPE': 'RSS', 'LAUNCH_DATE': 1646092800, 'SORT_ORDER': 0, 'BENCHMARK_SCORE': 0, 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'LAST_UPDATED_TS': 1764610921, 'CREATED_ON': 1709283963, 'UPDATED_ON': 1709284009}, 'CATEGORY_DATA': [{'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 6, 'NAME': 'ASIA', 'CATEGORY': 'ASIA'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 26, 'NAME': 'FIAT', 'CATEGORY': 'FIAT'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 37, 'NAME': 'MARKET', 'CATEGORY': 'MARKET'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 41, 'NAME': 'REGULATION', 'CATEGORY': 'REGULATION'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 210, 'NAME': 'MACROECONOMICS', 'CATEGORY': 'MACROECONOMICS'}]}, {'TYPE': '121', 'ID': 55209046, 'GUID': 'https://bitcoinworld.co.in/?p=224683', 'PUBLISHED_ON': 1764605411, 'PUBLISHED_ON_NS': None, 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/82/default.png', 'TITLE': 'Revealing: Coinbase Law Enforcement Requests Surge 19% as Global Scrutiny Intensifies', 'SUBTITLE': None, 'AUTHORS': 'Editorial Team', 'URL': 'https://bitcoinworld.co.in/coinbase-law-enforcement-requests-surge/', 'SOURCE_ID': 82, 'BODY': 'BitcoinWorld Revealing: Coinbase Law Enforcement Requests Surge 19% as Global Scrutiny Intensifies Have you ever wondered how often governments ask cryptocurrency exchanges for user data? The latest figures are in, and they paint a clear picture of escalating regulatory scrutiny. According to its 2025 transparency report, Coinbase law enforcement requests jumped by a significant 19% year-over-year. This surge highlights the growing intersection between the digital asset space and traditional legal frameworks. What Do the Latest Coinbase Law Enforcement Request Numbers Show? Between October 2024 and September 2025, Coinbase received a staggering 12,716 requests for cooperation from authorities worldwide. This marks a steady climb from previous years, signaling that law enforcement agencies are becoming more active in the crypto ecosystem. The exchange processes these requests carefully, aiming to balance legal obligations with its commitment to user privacy. Therefore, this trend is not isolated to one region. It reflects a global shift towards closer monitoring of cryptocurrency transactions. For users, this underscores the importance of understanding that while crypto offers pseudonymity, it is not entirely anonymous when interacting with regulated exchanges. Which Countries Are Driving the Increase in Requests? A deep dive into the data reveals a fascinating geographical breakdown. The United States remains the most frequent requester by far, accounting for over half (53%) of all Coinbase law enforcement requests . This dominance aligns with the country’s position as a major financial and regulatory hub. However, the most dramatic growth came from Europe. France, in particular, saw an astonishing 111% year-over-year increase in requests submitted to Coinbase. This suggests European authorities are rapidly scaling up their crypto-related investigative capabilities. United States: 53% of total requests (largest volume) France: 111% growth (largest percentage increase) Global Trend: Widespread increase across multiple jurisdictions Why Are Coinbase Law Enforcement Requests Rising So Sharply? Several key factors are driving this upward trend. First, the overall adoption of cryptocurrencies has expanded, leading to a larger user base and more transaction volume. Consequently, the potential for illicit activities, while a small percentage, represents a larger absolute number that attracts law enforcement attention. Second, regulatory frameworks worldwide are maturing. Governments are implementing clearer rules, and agencies are developing specialized units to track crypto flows. This institutional knowledge translates directly into more frequent and sophisticated data requests to major exchanges like Coinbase. What Does This Mean for the Future of Crypto and Privacy? This increase in Coinbase law enforcement requests is a double-edged sword. On one hand, it demonstrates the industry’s growing compliance with established legal systems, which can foster greater institutional trust and mainstream adoption. Exchanges that cooperate transparently help legitimize the entire sector. On the other hand, it raises important questions about financial privacy and the boundaries of surveillance. Users must recognize that using a centralized exchange involves entrusting a company with data that can be subpoenaed. The future will likely involve an ongoing negotiation between innovation, regulation, and individual rights. In summary, the 19% rise in Coinbase law enforcement requests is a powerful indicator of crypto’s journey into the regulatory mainstream. It reflects heightened global scrutiny, more capable authorities, and the industry’s evolving role within the traditional financial landscape. For investors and users, staying informed about these trends is crucial for navigating the space responsibly. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) What type of information do law enforcement agencies request from Coinbase? Typically, requests seek account holder information, transaction histories, and wallet addresses associated with specific investigations, often related to suspected fraud, money laundering, or other financial crimes. Does Coinbase notify users when their data is requested? Coinbase’s policy generally prohibits notifying users if doing so would compromise an investigation. However, they may inform users after a legal prohibition expires, unless legally barred. How does Coinbase validate these law enforcement requests? The exchange has a dedicated team that reviews each request for legal validity, ensuring it complies with applicable laws and their own privacy standards before releasing any data. Are all countries’ requests treated equally by Coinbase? No. Coinbase assesses requests based on the legal framework of the requesting country and its own policies. They may challenge or deny requests they deem overly broad or legally insufficient. What can users do to protect their privacy? Users should understand an exchange’s privacy policy, use strong security practices (like 2FA), and remember that on-chain activity, while pseudonymous, can be analyzed. For greater privacy, some users opt for decentralized platforms, which come with their own trade-offs. Is this trend unique to Coinbase? No, other major centralized exchanges report similar increases. This is an industry-wide phenomenon as regulators worldwide increase their focus on cryptocurrency markets. Found this insight into the rising tide of Coinbase law enforcement requests revealing? The conversation about crypto, regulation, and privacy is vital. Share this article on social media to help your network stay informed about these critical developments shaping the future of digital assets. To learn more about the latest cryptocurrency regulation trends, explore our article on key developments shaping global policies and institutional adoption. This post Revealing: Coinbase Law Enforcement Requests Surge 19% as Global Scrutiny Intensifies first appeared on BitcoinWorld .', 'KEYWORDS': 'Crypto News|COINBASE|Crypto Compliance|cryptocurrency regulation|law enforcement|transparency report', 'LANG': 'EN', 'UPVOTES': 0, 'DOWNVOTES': 0, 'SCORE': 0, 'SENTIMENT': 'NEUTRAL', 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'CREATED_ON': 1764605473, 'UPDATED_ON': None, 'SOURCE_DATA': {'TYPE': '120', 'ID': 82, 'SOURCE_KEY': 'bitcoinworld', 'NAME': 'Bitcoin World', 'IMAGE_URL': 'https://resources.cryptocompare.com/news/82/default.png', 'URL': 'https://bitcoinworld.co.in/feed/', 'LANG': 'EN', 'SOURCE_TYPE': 'RSS', 'LAUNCH_DATE': None, 'SORT_ORDER': 0, 'BENCHMARK_SCORE': 0, 'STATUS': 'ACTIVE', 'LAST_UPDATED_TS': 1764610938, 'CREATED_ON': 1698066984, 'UPDATED_ON': 1747932437}, 'CATEGORY_DATA': [{'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 13, 'NAME': 'BLOCKCHAIN', 'CATEGORY': 'BLOCKCHAIN'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID': 15, 'NAME': 'BUSINESS', 'CATEGORY': 'BUSINESS'}, {'TYPE': '122', 'ID'